On Thu, 24 Jan 2007, azgor wrote:
>
> [1]: Rock traps aren't strong enough in later levels. Implement falling
> boulder traps. Due to the large size of boulders, they can only hold
> one. --JoshJ

   Meh. Who says we need tough traps anyway? Maybe the answer is to
/take out/ rock traps entirely; or (more reasonably) prevent them
from appearing after a certain point; or just leave well enough alone.
I think rolling boulder traps are /way/ more interesting, and there
aren't very many of them in the dungeon; maybe there should be more.
   Of course, if you clutter up the dungeon with boulders (rolling or
fallen), you make navigation more tedious.

> [7]: Leprochauns should be able to turn straw golems into gold golems.
> --assended
>
> Uhm.... I thought that was Rumplestitskin.  There are no
> Rumplestiltskins in the game that I'm aware of.  If there were any,
> then they definitely should have this ability.  Was Rumplestiltskin a
> gnome?  I guess there could be a random unique gnome named
> Rumplestiltskin that has this ability, but how often would this come up
> if so?

   Yeah, I can't see how this would work. The most Nethackish mechanic
I can think of is that a player polymorphed into a leprechaun should
use the #turn command to turn straw golems within LOS and radius 5
into gold golems. (#turn normally means "turn undead"; IMHO it's a
very obscure extended command, even more than #wipe.)
   Main problem has already been pointed out: Rumpelstiltskin isn't
a leprechaun, he's just a random "little man" from the fairy tale.
(Elf, brownie, mannikin, whatever; but not the Irish type with the
brogue and the pot o' gold.)
   Another problem is that Rumpelstiltskin didn't "turn" straw into
gold; he specifically /spun/ it into gold on a spinning wheel. Which
we don't have in the game, either.

