> [9]: Items that are used in actions requiring confirmation (i.e. corpse
> sacrifice) should avoid being assigned to item letter 'y' and 'Y' to
> avoid accidental #pray + y instead of #offer + y. (idea by assended)
> --Eidolos
>
> Sounds like a non-trivial coding task to me, but what do I know.

   Sounds pretty trivial to me, but I've already got a long list of
trivial patches I haven't found time to implement yet. :)  It's made
simpler by the fact that once an item gets assigned a letter, that
letter does not change, to avoid confusing the user; so we should
preserve that behavior. Then all the YANI is, is: any time you're
about to assign something the letter 'y', check to see whether another
letter is available, and if so, use it instead. End of YANI. Easy.

> [10]: New creature inhabiting some gehennom levels, afraid of light
> sources but can cause instadeath. Upon arriving on a level with such a
> creature, one might get the message "It is dark in here. You might be
> eaten by a grue." Makes Sunsword more valuable, and saving a magic lamp
> or saving potions of oil and an oil lamp. --Yui

   Don't forget to make it really buggy and get lots of people to
play it in the tournament.</swipe>  Seriously, new instadeaths are
stupid. If it weren't an instadeath, and had an interesting AI or
interesting interactions with other mechanics, it might be worth
creating a new monster; but if your only idea is "let's rip off
Zork for a YAFM that's amusing exactly once", um, no, let's not.

> More instadeath!?  Why not just YAFM when you enter a dark level
> without a light source?

   Because that happens literally dozens of times per game. YAFMs
can't be seen more than *once every dozen games*, or they lose
the F and become YAMs, or worse, YAAMs.

> [11]: If you're a canine, you should not see in color. --BenUrban
>
> THAT is funny!  Sounds like a PITA for the coder and the player,
> though.  If anyone wants to write it, I'd consider installing it
> locally, myself... but I'm weird.  Actually, don't they think that most
> animals have little color vision?  I think bees see in
> black/white+ultraviolet, for instance.

   Meh. In my rationalization, the color displayed on screen doesn't
really correspond to color in the game world anyway; it's just a way
of getting an extra 3 or 4 bits of information into each glyph. Even
without color vision, a dog would be able to tell the difference between
a dwarf and a hobgoblin from a distance. Removing the visual cues in
the display is just a PITA for the player, as azgor says.

